---
/**
 * Original BotPlanet explainer — what a mower's area rating actually promises.
 *
 * THE MISREADING THIS HUB OPENS ON. "Every machine states a maximum it can
 * maintain, and that figure assumes it mows most days — a mower rated for an
 * acre does not cut an acre in an afternoon, it keeps an acre tidy over a week."
 * That sentence is doing a lot of work and it loses, because the reader arrives
 * with a petrol mower in their head: one long push, once a week, a bin full of
 * clippings. The rating gets read as capacity per cut when it is capacity per
 * week, and somebody who reads it that way buys a machine rated at exactly their
 * lawn and then wonders why it never stops running.
 *
 * Drawn as two weeks side by side, because the difference IS the week — no
 * single moment shows it. The consequence sits underneath where it belongs:
 * headroom is not luxury, it is the difference between a mower that idles and a
 * mower that runs constantly to keep up.
 *
 * Conceptual. No machine is named, and no area, runtime or cutting width is
 * printed — those are per-model figures and they belong on a review beside the
 * maker who published them.
 */
---
<figure class="dgm">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 640 320" width="100%" role="img"
    aria-label="Two weeks compared. A push mower makes one long cut on one day and the grass grows back between cuts. A robot mower makes a short cut on most days, so the grass never gets long. The area a robot is rated for is what it can keep tidy over a week, not what it can cut in one session.">
    <rect x="0" y="0" width="640" height="320" rx="14" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--border)"/>

    <text x="24" y="34" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#f5f5f7">The rating is per week, not per cut</text>
    <text x="24" y="54" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">A mower rated for an acre keeps an acre tidy. It does not cut an acre in an afternoon.</text>

    {/* THE DAYS RUN THROUGH BOTH CHARTS. Two plots stacked with one axis at the
        bottom read as two unrelated pictures; the whole argument is that the
        same week produces different grass. */}
    <g stroke="var(--border-2)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 5" opacity="0.55">
      <line x1="88" y1="92" x2="88" y2="262"/>
      <line x1="173" y1="92" x2="173" y2="262"/>
      <line x1="258" y1="92" x2="258" y2="262"/>
      <line x1="343" y1="92" x2="343" y2="262"/>
      <line x1="428" y1="92" x2="428" y2="262"/>
      <line x1="513" y1="92" x2="513" y2="262"/>
      <line x1="598" y1="92" x2="598" y2="262"/>
    </g>

    <!-- ============ the push mower week ============ -->
    <text x="24" y="84" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#8b8b95">A mower you push</text>

    {/* GRASS HEIGHT, HELD INSIDE A BAND. The growth curve used to climb clear
        out of its own plot and finish level with the title, which put the words
        "one long cut" above the standfirst and made the two charts impossible
        to compare — the thing the drawing exists to do. Both plots now share
        one scale: y=100 is long, y=160 is short. */}
    <line x1="88" y1="160" x2="598" y2="160" stroke="var(--border-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
    <path d="M88 158 L173 141 L258 128 L343 118 L428 110 L513 104 L513 156 L598 142"
      fill="none" stroke="#8b8b95" stroke-width="2.5"/>
    {/* The cut lands on Sunday, where the drop is drawn. */}
    <text x="524" y="96" font-size="10.5" fill="#8b8b95">one long cut</text>
    <text x="24" y="104" font-size="10.5" fill="#8b8b95">long</text>
    <text x="24" y="164" font-size="10.5" fill="#8b8b95">short</text>

    <!-- ============ the robot week ============ -->
    <text x="24" y="212" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#4fd1c5">A robot mower</text>

    <line x1="88" y1="262" x2="598" y2="262" stroke="var(--border-2)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
    <path d="M88 260 L130 250 L130 260 L215 250 L215 260 L300 250 L300 260 L385 250 L385 260 L470 250 L470 260 L555 250 L555 260 L598 254"
      fill="none" stroke="#4fd1c5" stroke-width="2.5"/>
    <text x="24" y="240" font-size="10.5" fill="#8b8b95">long</text>
    <text x="24" y="266" font-size="10.5" fill="#8b8b95">short</text>

    <!-- day markers, shared by both plots above -->
    <g font-size="10" fill="#8b8b95" text-anchor="middle">
      <text x="88" y="282">Mon</text>
      <text x="173" y="282">Tue</text>
      <text x="258" y="282">Wed</text>
      <text x="343" y="282">Thu</text>
      <text x="428" y="282">Fri</text>
      <text x="513" y="282">Sat</text>
      <text x="598" y="282">Sun</text>
    </g>

    <text x="24" y="306" font-size="11" fill="#c9c9d1">Buy one rated close to your lawn and it runs constantly to keep up. Buy with headroom and it works less to do more.</text>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="note">
    A robot takes a little off on most days rather than a lot on one, which is why the clippings are
    short enough to fall back into the lawn and why the maximum area is a weekly figure. Compare the
    rating with your lawn and leave room above it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .dgm { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .dgm svg { max-width: 640px; display: block; }
  .dgm .note { margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
